{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 41, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that delves into the origins of facebook and the complex web of ambition, betrayal, and innovation that surrounded its creation. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the movie is not just a biopic—it’s a sharp, fast-paced drama that feels more like a courtroom thriller wrapped in the skin of a tech origin story.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him as brilliant, socially awkward, and emotionally detached. his portrayal captures the paradox of a young man who builds a global platform for connection yet struggles to form genuine human bonds. the supporting cast is equally compelling: andrew garfield as edward sneedler, the loyal friend turned bitter rival, and jesse plemons as the quiet, calculating connor sneedler, both bring depth and nuance to characters who are more than just foils to zuckerberg.\n\naaron sorkin’s screenplay is a tour de force—witty, dense, and relentlessly rhythmic. the dialogue crackles with intelligence and tension, turning legal depositions into gripping narrative devices. the film’s non-linear structure, alternating between two lawsuits and the rise of facebook, keeps the viewer engaged as the pieces of the puzzle slowly fall into place.\n\ndavid fincher’s direction is precise and atmospheric. every frame feels meticulously composed, with a cold, blue-gray palette that mirrors the emotional detachment of the characters. the score by trent reznor and atticus ross is haunting and electronic, perfectly encapsulating the digital age’s pulse and the loneliness beneath the screen.\n\nthe film doesn’t take sides. it doesn’t glorify zuckerberg nor vilify him. instead, it presents a sobering portrait of how innovation can be born from resentment, how friendship can be commodified, and how the pursuit of perfection can come at the cost of humanity. it’s a story about power, isolation, and the unintended consequences of connecting the world—while remaining disconnected yourself.\n\nthe social network isn’t just about facebook. it’s about the modern condition: the tension between genius and loneliness, between creation and consequence. it’s one of the most intelligent, compelling, and enduring films of the 2010s, and it remains as relevant today as it was upon its release.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0003810394846368581}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.4176788032054901}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.37157994508743286}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0009815642843022943}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.0058195204474031925}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00013195598148740828}, {"token": " del", "logprob": -0.9510456919670105}, {"token": "ves", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -0.007622679229825735}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.1920922133867862e-06}, {"token": " origins", "logprob": -0.67808997631073}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.731181277544238e-05}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.00019643761334009469}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -1.0587118864059448}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " complex", "logprob": -0.578078031539917}, {"token": " web", "logprob": -1.1419262886047363}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " ambition", "logprob": -1.156235933303833}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " betrayal", "logprob": -0.014317640103399754}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.005303835961967707}, {"token": " innovation", "logprob": -0.7119541168